Post Date: 2015-12-04T09:45:35Z
Current Date: April 4 2025
Title: onErrorResumeNext/OperatorOnErrorResumeNextViaFunction ignoring unsubscription?
Description: I first wanted to ask, just in case, to check if the behavior I'm going to describe is the expected.

It happens that using the operator `OperatorOnErrorResumeNextViaFunction` seems to ignore the **unsubscription** event.

I had this code for testing it.

``` java
        final AtomicBoolean readyToUnsubscribe = new AtomicBoolean(false);
        final AtomicBoolean readyToFail = new AtomicBoolean(false);
        final ReentrantLock lock = new ReentrantLock();
        final Condition rtuCond = lock.newCondition();
        final Condition rtfCond = lock.newCondition();


        Subscription subscribe = Observable.create(
                new Observable.OnSubscribe<String>() {
                    @Override
                    public void call(Subscriber<? super String> subscriber) {
                        lock.lock();
                        System.out.println("Subscription started: " + !subscriber.isUnsubscribed());
                        readyToUnsubscribe.set(true);
                        rtuCond.signal();
                        try {
                            while (!readyToFail.get()) {
                                rtfCond.await();
                            }
                        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                            Exceptions.throwIfFatal(e);
                        } finally {
                            lock.unlock();
                        }
                        System.out.println("Gonna fail");
                        throw new RuntimeException("Yup Crashing Bad!");
                    }
                }
        )
                .onErrorResumeNext(
                        new Func1<Throwable, Observable<? extends String>>() {
                            @Override
                            public Observable<? extends String> call(Throwable throwable) {
                                return Observable.just("Recovered from: " + throwable.getMessage());
                            }
                        }
                )
                .subscribeOn(Schedulers.io())
                .subscribe(
                        new Action1<String>() {
                            @Override
                            public void call(String s) {
                                System.out.println("Got message: " + s);
                            }
                        },
                        new Action1<Throwable>() {
                            @Override
                            public void call(Throwable throwable) {
                                throwable.printStackTrace(System.err);
                            }
                        },
                        new Action0() {
                            @Override
                            public void call() {
                                System.out.println("Completed");
                            }
                        }
                );

        lock.lock();
        while (!readyToUnsubscribe.get()) {
            rtuCond.await();
        }
        subscribe.unsubscribe();
        System.out.println("Now this is unsubscribed");
        readyToFail.set(false);
        rtfCond.signal();
        lock.unlock();
```

Which gives the output

```
Subscription started: true
Now this is unsubscribed
Gonna fail
Got message: Recovered from: Yup Crashing Bad!
Completed
```

And I would have expected none of the Obsever's methods to be called indeed, that is last two lines of output should't be there. But this is my understanding, can somebody confirm if this should be considered a bug, or is it expected behavior of the onErrorResumeNext operator?


